  "description": "Proof of concept: WRI's Aqueduct 4.0 layer served live from ArcGIS Online to the kepler.gl panel, with no prior download. Clipped to South America because global (61,488 polygons, 58 MiB, 171 s) does not fit in a dashboard. The map's pagination uses `replace` mode with ${__pagination.*} markers in the URL, to dodge an aliasing bug in Infinity 4.0.0 that makes `query` mode return the same page N times.",
  "editable": true,
  "fiscalYearStartMonth": 0,
  "graphTooltip": 0,
  "liveNow": false,
  "panels": [
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 9,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 1,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "# Water risk — WRI Aqueduct 4.0\n\nThe polygons are **HydroBASINS level 6 basins cut by province**, served live from the ArcGIS Online Feature Service, **with no prior download and no ETL**. Changing the indicator or the country queries the service again.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Source | [Aqueduct 4.0 Water Risk](https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=c784f4ebddaf43c8b816612fb62e7e5b) · **Aqueduct 4.0, World Resources Institute (WRI)**, hosted by Esri. Licensed CC BY 4.0 |\n| Coverage | 13 countries of South America · 6,469 units with data for water stress (out of 6,717) |\n| How it arrives | Infinity → `f=geojson` → a `geojson` column → the panel detects it and kepler draws it |\n\n**Three things to know so as not to misread the map:**\n\n- **The gaps are \"No Data\".** Aqueduct encodes a missing value as `-9999`, and a single `-9999` would\n  wreck the colour scale, so they are filtered at source (`$​{indicador}_score >= 0`). What is missing\n  from the map is data that does not exist, not zero risk.\n- **\"Arid and Low Water Use\" scores high by convention**, not from measured stress: these are 493 arid\n  units with little water use. They come out red without there being any competition for the resource.\n- **Level 6 is coarse.** It works for a national or regional read. It does not work at municipal scale.\n\n**Filtering by drawing on the map.** With the map's draw tool (polygon or rectangle) you can narrow the\nanalysis: the ranking by country, the table and the \"In selection\" counter come to describe only the\nbasins the shape touches. **The map deliberately does not filter itself** — it goes on showing the whole\nextent as context, because if its own query depended on the shape, shrinking the dataset would leave no\nway to widen it again from the map. To go back to the total, delete the shape.\n\n*If \"With data\" and the number of polygons on the map disagree, Infinity's pagination is truncating: it\nneeds `GF_PLUGIN_PAGINATION_MAX_PAGES` in the container.*",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.2.0",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "",
      "transparent": true,
      "type": "text"
    },
